" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1045 "Properties of Diagonalization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "95082e0a-322a-5587-9f1a-1280f50a5124" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125058 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125058 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125058 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37314" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125058 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125058 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $F$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125058 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b5330de-42e9-59fc-85b7-be3de824baa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125058 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $F,$ then $F$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $F=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $F,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nF={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125419 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125419 #edb/ref 1061 20121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125419 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "934d1edd-bf1c-5c1f-8924-ede352d7d23b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125419 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125420 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125420 #edb/ref 1061 20122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125420 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d76255af-54d9-5dcb-9a9c-7b30b6c4a98a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125420 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125421 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125421 #edb/ref 1061 20123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125421 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ed440e4-0b21-56bd-92c6-0ef75f28a68c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125421 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125422 #edb/ref 1061 20124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125422 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "476486fe-26df-57a3-89ab-80eeb17483b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125422 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125492 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125492 #edb/ref 1061 26234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125492 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1265d446-04a7-5d76-a5b8-e7c56bb1b4c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125492 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125790 #edb/ref 1064 "One useful property of diagonalization is that it allows us to easily determine the power of a matrix.\n\nAs an example, we consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix ![](images/53/53b7a9efd9f2ae1ef4adc7cee5f86f784722777b8c29f268dbd0e822ff1354f9.svg) with the diagonalization\n![](images/e3/e3111a91b80d238cf5255eb9a9884dcd3f80e914039c5e789e32c07b55cf5b06.svg)\n\nWe will use the diagonalization of $A$ to compute $A^{55}.$\n\nLet's start by computing $A^2.$ Using the properties of matrix multiplication, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =PD{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =PD\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P(DD){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $A^3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot A \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P({D}^{2}D){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{3}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix $P$ and its inverse $P^{-1}$ will always cancel each other out in the middle of the multiplication! So by induction, we have that\n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}55}=P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}55} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we only need to find $D^{\\color{blue}55}.$\n\nSince $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {D}^{3} & ={D}^{2}D=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing this pattern, we get\n\n![](images/05/05e10fdc6944077a48b0214a41fae8d418a1b66c8d87a47d652158c84285c246.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{55} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{55}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given that $A = PDP^{-1},$ for any non-negative integer ${\\color{blue}k},$ we obtain \n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}k} = P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}k} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125790 #edb/ref 1065 3576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125790 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b7628bf0-02d4-514e-a564-669610253cd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125790 #edb/ref 1067 "Raising a Diagonalizable Matrix to a Power" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125791 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that the diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ and corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ is\n![](images/cd/cde63e04857414720bed0cb247e1e0c4bae76b69823f4aeb494cda13c139ac13.svg)\n\nThis idea also works in reverse. Given a diagonalization $A$ in the form \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe can immediately deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are the diagonal entries of $D,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of $P.$ This is because the diagonalization of a matrix is (almost) unique.\n\n**Note:** The diagonalization is \"almost\" unique because the order of the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs may vary, and the eigenvectors may be replaced with scalar multiples of themselves. However, none of these variations change the actual outcome regarding the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs that we deduce." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125791 #edb/ref 1065 3577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125791 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d23e5c1b-9483-574b-ab9f-f68b126993cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125791 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269293 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 9 \\\\ -24 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269293 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269293 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02c36a09-640e-5f2f-bee6-5e3ec7470874" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269705 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 9 \\\\ -21 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269705 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269705 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3064e2aa-2353-5c23-8c5a-c90a2e00dae4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269921 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269921 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269921 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "47e19908-9201-5477-8a4f-5d2e1bcb5a23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270252 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 6 \\\\ -12 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186270252 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270252 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6eb22d41-12a9-5433-9689-c05376bd7c15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270536 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 6 \\\\ -18 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186270536 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270536 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "918b8499-30b8-5523-aa6d-ee8503603d